# InnBucks cell — per-cell provisioning policy (NON-SECRET)
#
# Copy to cell.<iso>.env, edit for the market, and source it before running
# provision-cell.sh:
#
#     set -a; source deploy/cells/cell.zw.env; set +a
#     set -a; source deploy/fineract/.env;     set +a     # secrets, gitignored
#     ./deploy/fineract/provision-cell.sh
#
# > THIS REPO IS PUBLIC. Nothing in this file may be a secret, a hostname, an
# > IP, or anything else that targets a machine. Passwords and tokens live in
# > the gitignored .env only. What belongs here is POLICY: role names,
# > permission codes, GL codes — the stuff that should be identical every time
# > this market's cell is stood up, and reviewable as a diff.
#
# Everything here is applied IDEMPOTENTLY and ADDITIVELY. Re-running the script
# re-asserts this file's intent; it never revokes a permission it doesn't name,
# because stripping a grant an operator added deliberately is worse than
# leaving one behind.

# ─── Cell identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CELL_CURRENCY=USD
PRODUCT_NAME="InnBucks Wallet"
PRODUCT_SHORT=IBWL

# ─── CBS console dropdowns (step 4d) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults in the script are ZIMBABWE-shaped. Override per market. These become
# m_client_non_person foreign keys — renaming one after real companies
# reference it is a migration, not an edit.
# CLIENT_CONSTITUTIONS="Sole Trader|Partnership|..."
# CLIENT_BUSINESS_LINES="Agriculture|Mining|..."

# ─── Bank operational roles (step 6b) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Format:  "Role Name=CODE1,CODE2;Other Role=CODE3"
#          ';' between roles, '=' before the codes, ',' between codes.
# The role must ALREADY EXIST — roles are created by the bank, never here. A
# name that matches nothing is a loud refusal naming the closest existing roles,
# because the alternative is worse than useless: creating it yields an empty
# role no operator is assigned to, granted perfectly, reported as success, while
# the person it was meant for still cannot do the thing. (That happened on the
# ZW cell: "Test Internal Auditor" against a cell whose role is "TEST Internal
# Audit".) Codes are granted ADDITIVELY and every code is checked against the
# running build first, so a typo warns rather than silently producing a role
# that authenticates fine and is refused everywhere.
#
# Get the exact names — including case — from the cell itself:
#   api GET /v1/roles | jq -r '.[]|"\(.id)\t\(.name)"'
# and check who actually holds them, since a grant to a role with no members
# changes nothing for anyone:
#   api GET /v1/users | jq -r '.[]|"\(.username)\t\([.selectedRoles[]?.name]|join(", "))"'
BANK_ROLE_GRANTS=""

# ─── Maker-checker (step 6e) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tasks to place under dual control. Codes are '<ACTION>_<ENTITY>'.
#
# Flagging a task is HALF the job: the checker's role also needs the matching
# '<CODE>_CHECKER' grant (put it in BANK_ROLE_GRANTS above), or their Checker
# Inbox stays empty even when commands park correctly.
#
# > Do not go looking for those codes in GET /v1/permissions — they are not
# > there. That query ends "where code not like '%\_CHECKER'", so all 249 seeded
# > checker permissions are invisible to it, and to the console screen built on
# > it. GET /v1/roles/{id}/permissions is the unfiltered listing. (provision-cell.sh
# > reads that one; it used to read the filtered one and wrongly reported every
# > checker code as not existing.)
#
# NOTE these five actions have NO seeded _CHECKER permission row, so only a
# CHECKER_SUPER_USER could approve them: UNDOWRITEOFF_LOAN, RECOVERYPAYMENT_LOAN,
# REVERSE_JOURNALENTRY, ADJUSTTRANSACTION_SAVINGSACCOUNT, UNDO_WAIVECHARGE.
# (Verified against the seed changelogs 2026-09-18 — 912 codes, 249 checkers.)
MAKER_CHECKER_TASKS=""

# Flip the GLOBAL maker-checker switch. Seeded OFF by Fineract, and while it is
# off every per-task flag above is inert — which is exactly why "Configure
# Maker Checker Tasks" in the console appears to work and changes nothing.
#
# > Do NOT set this to 1 until the console handles a PARKED response. A parked
# > command answers 200 {"commandId":N,"rollbackTransaction":true} with no
# > resourceId and NOTHING applied — a UI that reads 200 as success will report
# > "posted!" for a rolled-back journal.
#
# The script ALWAYS asserts the middleware's own permission codes are unflagged
# before it enables this, whatever you set here.
ENABLE_MAKER_CHECKER=0

# ─── GL control accounts (step 6c) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# '|'-separated glCodes that must refuse manual journal entries
# (manualEntriesAllowed=false). Typically the loan portfolio, savings control,
# interest/fee receivable and suspense accounts.
#
# Keyed on glCode, never on id: ids differ per cell, glCodes are the chart.
CONTROL_ACCOUNT_GLCODES=""

# ─── Loan product accounting (step 6d) ───────────────────────────────────────
# The script ASSERTS every loan product has accounting enabled; it does not
# author the GL mappings (6-8 account ids per product — author those once in
# the console). Set to 0 to silence the check on a cell that deliberately runs
# products without accounting.
#
# Why it is checked at all: a write-off on a product whose accounting rule is
# None SUCCEEDS and posts NO journal entries, silently. That is not a failure
# anyone notices until the GL is reconciled.
ASSERT_LOAN_PRODUCT_ACCOUNTING=1
